Hi!

Recently I spent three hours discussing nethack with a friend who wanted to
try to develope a mutli-player version of nethack.  He came up with ideas
and I promptly rejected them.  He modified his plans to accomodate my
objections and then I told him why I didn't like his new idea either.  This
process went on for quite some time and I thought it might be interesting
to report where it finally ended up.

At first his idea started to sound like a MUD, and I didn't like that at all.
As he kept on modifying and modifying his ideas, it became less MUD like
(which I thought was good).  For a while it started to become a lot like that
arcade game "gauntlet", but if I wanted to play an arcade game, I would go
out to an arcade. (We actually used to go out to the local bowling alley and
each of the four of us would be armed with a roll of quarters and we would
take over that game.  I wasn't very good though; I always ran out of quarters
first).  So it moved away from being like gauntlet and after a while, as more
and more difficulties were removed, it ended up as:

Advanced Dungeons & Dragons.  One of the things was the question, "Why bother
to have multiple players at the same time?  What is that going to add to the
game?  Surely not just to make it easier to play-- if you wanted that then
you should just improve things like what each player starts with.  So there
must be something else."  It turned out that the something else evolved into
"being able to have more depth by acting in coordination with other people".
Eventually it was decided that a complex mechanism was required to handle
a game of the necessary depth to make use of things like player cooperation 
and problem solving, and vol-ia! Get rid of the computer and get a good
DM.  (Oh, early on it was decided that the game should have a limited number
of players and they should be all familiar with each other, ie not playing
from all different parts of the country at one time).

Now, not all chains of thought like this will end up the way that ours did,
but I thought it would be instructive to see what ours led to.  Certainly I
can see other such chains evolving into other forms of games.  But I am
going to be dusting off my books (I have all of them, and a Monster Manual
that was printed in 1979) and looking for my 20, 12, 10, 8, 6, and 4 sided
dice.  Of course I will still be hacking, but I'll be doing that alone.

